# Angular RSS 2025 - AI Development Context & Rules

## Project Overview

This is an Angular 20.1.0 RSS reader application built with TypeScript, following modern Angular patterns and best practices.

## Angular Development Best Practices (Based on Angular.dev Guidelines)

### TypeScript Configuration

- Always use `strict: true` type checking
- Prefer type inference when obvious
- Avoid `any`; use `unknown` with type guards instead
- Use proper interfaces and types for RSS data structures

### Modern Angular Patterns (Angular 20+)

- **Always use standalone components** (implicit `standalone: true`)
- Use **signals** and `computed()` for reactive state management
- Implement `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` for performance
- Keep components small and single-responsibility
- Use `input()` and `output()` functions instead of decorators
- Prefer inline templates for small components

### Template Syntax (New Control Flow)

- Use **native control flow**: `@if`, `@for`, `@switch` instead of `*ngIf`, `*ngFor`, `*ngSwitch`
- Use `[class]` and `[style]` bindings instead of `ngClass` and `ngStyle`
- Use `async` pipe for observables
- Avoid complex logic in templates

### Service Patterns

- Design services with single responsibility
- Use `providedIn: 'root'` for singleton services
- Use `inject()` function instead of constructor injection
- Implement proper error handling for RSS feeds

### State Management

- Use **signals** for local component state
- Use `computed()` for derived values
- Keep state pure and predictable
- Use RxJS only when necessary (HTTP calls, complex async operations)

### Performance & Assets

- Use `NgOptimizedImage` for all static images
- Implement lazy loading for feature routes
- Use OnPush change detection strategy
- Implement virtual scrolling for large RSS feed lists

## RSS-Specific Development Patterns

### Data Models

```typescript
interface RSSFeed {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  url: string;
  description?: string;
  lastUpdated: Date;
  isActive: boolean;
}

interface RSSItem {
  id: string;
  feedId: string;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  link: string;
  publishDate: Date;
  isRead: boolean;
}
```

### Service Architecture

- **FeedService**: Centralized RSS feed management with signals
- **RSSParserService**: XML parsing and format handling
- **StorageService**: Local storage and caching
- **ErrorHandlerService**: Global error handling

### Component Structure

App Component (Root)
├── Header Component
├── Sidebar Component (Feed Navigation)
├── Main Content Area
│ ├── Feed List Component
│ ├── Article List Component
│ └── Article Detail Component
└── Footer Component

## Code Quality Standards

### ESLint Rules (Angular-Specific)

- Follow @angular-eslint/eslint-plugin recommended rules
- Prefer `inject()` over constructor injection
- Use standalone components
- Implement OnPush change detection
- Use signals over reactive forms where appropriate

### Testing Patterns

- Write unit tests for all services
- Test component interactions
- Mock HTTP calls for RSS feeds
- Test error scenarios (malformed feeds, network errors)

### File Naming Conventions

- Use dash-delimited file names with type indicators
- Example: `feed-manager.component.ts`, `rss-parser.service.ts`
- Components: `*.component.ts`
- Services: `*.service.ts`
- Models: `*.model.ts` or `*.interface.ts`

## Security Considerations

- Sanitize RSS content before display using Angular's DomSanitizer
- Validate RSS feed URLs
- Implement Content Security Policy (CSP)
- Handle CORS issues with proxy configuration

## Performance Optimization

- Implement caching for RSS feeds
- Use virtual scrolling for large article lists
- Lazy load feature modules
- Optimize bundle size with tree shaking
- Use service workers for offline functionality

